  "name": "Ranger Dragonhawk Tamer summons one combat guardian per Horn for 15 seconds (#2174)",
  "contract": [
    "Dragonhawk Tamer 573056 is the obtainable Ranger Survival talent at CharacterAdvancement 34199. Its visible client contract promises a Dragonhawk whenever a Horn is used, for $573058d. The passive has aura 42 triggering 573058, 100% chance and no DBC proc flags; the world has no spell_proc row. 573058 summons entry 52393 with SummonProperties 61 and SpellDuration 8 (15000 ms), but the creature is absent.",
    "The four active Horns 800086, 800088, 806359 and 806360 all carry family 27, family flags (0,0,1), DmgClass MAGIC and are positive casts. A CAST-phase proc fires once for the caster, rather than once for each party recipient. Their ordinary shared category 1253 lasts 60000 ms; a rejected second Horn must not summon another creature. Hunting Quiver 800262 is a positive family-27 magic cast with different flags and must not summon a Dragonhawk.",
    "Preserved ascension-data cache snapshot 371f9d8db5a9d987f41be19b91fb87873d75654780243c9bd21c110852be0424, enUS/coa-beta creaturecache payload a6648462c0e523ccc110b27c67cbddbec0f64c19ee0aee9d27046f2cfe6e3adc, identifies 52393 as Dragonhawk, Beast, model 17545 with HealthModifier/ManaModifier 1. The existing Ranger companion hook derives weapon damage and attack power from owner-level creature_classlevelstats for 52393, as for War Falcons. The guardian must assist ordinary owner combat, still exist after ten seconds, and leave by sixteen seconds with native update tolerance.",
    "Talent acquisition goes through specialization 29 and native advancement uploads, without learning the passive directly. Four talented Rangers share a party with an untalented control: party recipients must not proc for someone else's Horn. The untalented Ranger's ordinary Horn and the talented Ranger's unrelated Hunting Quiver are negative controls. Proc and summon counts, native shared-cooldown rejection, owner-level weapon damage, enemy damage after the owner stops attacking, and expiration are observed independently."
  ],
